      "history_summaries": [
        "Round 1: Fixed DUT board initialization bug in field5.scala (lines 31-35). Replaced `RegInit(VecInit(Seq.fill(25)(1.U(1.W))))` + two separate `board(12):=0.U` / `board(24):=0.U` element assignments with a single `RegInit(VecInit((0 until 25).map(i => if (i==12||i==24) 0.U else 1.U)))`. The old element-wise connections were dead code overridden by `board := nextBoard` at line 112, causing all 25 positions to be 1. The fix ensures holes 12 (center) and 24 (corner) are initialized to 0 inside RegInit so they survive the whole-vector update. This addresses the causal path: the all-1s board caused center_empty, corner_empty, and all four move-boundary assertions to fail because board(from)===1.U was true for every input position. Preserved all assertion labels unchanged. Homologous assertion: corner_empty (same structural pattern as center_empty — both check empty-hole invariant for specific board positions).",
        "Round 2: Removed two incorrect assertions 'center_empty' (line 124-125) and 'corner_empty' (line 127-128) that both checked board(position)===0.U for positions 12 and 24 respectively. These positions start empty in the initial configuration but can be legitimately filled during normal peg solitaire gameplay — e.g., a RIGHT move from position 10 jumps over position 11 and lands in position 12 (center). The waveform shows this exact scenario: board[12] transitions from 0→1 after a valid move sequence, which is correct DUT behavior. The assertions were not valid invariants of the design. Both assertions are structurally identical (same pattern: specific-board-position===0.U) and have been removed together. Assertion labels 'center_empty' and 'corner_empty' cannot be preserved meaningfully since the property they encode is fundamentally incorrect for the peg solitaire game. Remaining assertions (cnt_range, cnt_decreases_by_one, left/right/up/down_move_boundary) are preserved unchanged."
      ],
